WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:06.520
Okay, so moving on to the next use case for the views.

00:06.520 --> 00:11.120
We can use it in order to have more dynamic and flexibility in our projects.

00:11.120 --> 00:12.800
So let's understand what this means.

00:12.800 --> 00:17.440
If you have a table and you have multiple users accessing this table, now, what can happen?

00:17.440 --> 00:22.400
You might change your mind about the design and the data model of your database, where you can say,

00:22.400 --> 00:27.200
you know what, instead of having one table, I'm going to go and split it into two tables, or maybe

00:27.200 --> 00:27.960
another decision.

00:27.960 --> 00:32.120
You say, you know what, I'm going to go and rename it table or in another day you decide, you know

00:32.120 --> 00:36.040
what, let's go and rename few columns or maybe add a column remove column.

00:36.040 --> 00:40.600
So you are doing changes to your physical data model and you are changing stuff in the tables.

00:40.600 --> 00:41.920
You know what's going to happen.

00:42.160 --> 00:48.240
All those users that are accessing the tables are gonna scream, because all of them having a complex

00:48.240 --> 00:54.280
SQL queries and your small changes at the tables are breaking everything in their queries.

00:54.280 --> 00:55.360
And what this means.

00:55.360 --> 01:01.840
This means escalations and you don't have anymore the freedom to change anything in your database without

01:01.920 --> 01:04.880
talking before to a hundred people before doing any change.

01:04.880 --> 01:06.040
So we don't do that.

01:06.040 --> 01:08.210
Instead of that we use views.

01:08.210 --> 01:09.130
So what's going to happen?

01:09.130 --> 01:14.210
You create a view and you tell the users, okay, take this view and consume it and leave me alone.

01:14.210 --> 01:17.930
And now you have again your freedom to do any changes you want.

01:17.970 --> 01:22.810
So you go to your tables and do splitting, renaming and changing everything you want.

01:23.050 --> 01:29.610
As long as you are updating the query between the table and the view to make sure that the users are

01:29.610 --> 01:31.010
not noticing any change.

01:31.010 --> 01:35.450
So for example, if you go and split the table into two tables, then you have to put in the view a

01:35.490 --> 01:41.490
join or a union in order to reconstruct the same structure that the users are used to.

01:41.530 --> 01:46.210
And if you would like to rename something on your database like instead of ID, you are now calling

01:46.210 --> 01:46.890
it a key.

01:46.930 --> 01:53.250
Only what you have to do now is to go to the query of the view and rename it back from a key to an ID,

01:53.250 --> 01:57.570
so no one can notice that you are doing changes to the physical tables.

01:57.570 --> 02:01.810
So using views and offering it to users is a game changer for you.

02:01.810 --> 02:08.290
Because giving the users views gives you more freedom, dynamic and flexibility to change anything in

02:08.290 --> 02:11.770
your data model and tables without getting any headache.

02:11.770 --> 02:13.850
So this is amazing use case for the views.
